==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|appertainance||dated}}
* {{alter|en|appurtenaunce||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|appurtenaunce}}, from {{der|en|xno|apurtenance}} and {{der|en|fro|apartenance}}, from {{m|fro|apartenir}}, from {{der|en|la|appertineō||I belong, I appertain}}.  Equivalent to {{suffix|en|appertain|ance}}. More at {{l|en|appertain}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Northern California, US}} {{audio|en|En-us-ncalif-appurtenance.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# That which [[appertain]]s; an [[appendage]] to something else; an [[addition]].
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Donkey|page=17|passage=Indeed, that was as it should be; for she was only an '''appurtenance''' of my mattress, or self-acting bedstead on four castors.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|volume=I|page=36|passage=The youngsters, not immediately within sight, seemed rather bright and desirable '''appurtenances''' than otherwise; the incidents of daily life were not without humorousness and jollity in their aspect there.}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} [[equipment|Equipment]] used for some specific [[task]]; [[gear]].
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Steven Poole|authorlink=Steven Poole|url=http://www.theguardian.com/books/2013/jul/06/nature-writing-revival|title=Is our love of nature writing bourgeois escapism?|date=2013-07-06|work=The Guardian|passage=In the case of the urban consumer of nature writing, of course, the mud is to be hosed off one's mental Range Rover immediately one lifts one's eyes from the page and gives silent thanks for the civilised '''appurtenances''' of hot yoga and flat whites.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Teddy Wayne|authorlink=Teddy Wayne|title=A Parchment on Millennials|work=The New York Times|date=2014-08-02|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/03/opinion/sunday/a-parchment-on-millennials.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=These “millennials” are, without a doubt, the most narcissistic and hopeless cohort I have witnessed in my 35 long years on this stationary planet. Consider, first, their absurd sartorial '''appurtenances''', such as leggings sewn with skinny girths.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[subordinate]] [[interest in land]] which [[benefits]] a [[principal]] estate, which cannot be [[detached]] from or held [[separately]] to that estate; an appurtenant interest.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1908| title=Water Rights in the Western States|author=Samuel Charles Wiel | page=340| passage=It is well settled that a water right may pass with land as an '''appurtenance''' thereto, or as a parcel thereof.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019| title=Lovell our Dogge|author=Michèle Schindler | page=| passage=Also I will that anon after the said nine years after my decease determined that my said feoffees make estate of all the said manors, lands and tenements with the '''appurtenance''' to my next heir and to the heirs of his body lawfully begotten.}}
#: {{syn|en|contenement}}
# {{lb|en|grammar}} A [[modifier]] that is [[append]]ed or [[prepend]]ed to another word to [[coin]] a new word that expresses belonging.
# The state or quality of being an appurtenance.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1970| title=Outer Continental Shelf|author=United States. Congress. Senate. Interior and Insular Affairs | page=275| passage=Thus, these two characteristics — '''appurtenance''' and the lack of need for any act of acquisition - go together and depend upon each other; one is merely looking at different facets of the same thing. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005| title=Heinz Werner and Developmental Science |author=Jaan Valsiner | page=262| passage=A second father is Kurt Koffka for his principle of '''appurtenance''' that he expanded from motions to colors.}}
# {{rfv-sense|en}} The thing to which another [[pertain]]s.

